---
import {
  ASSUMPTIONS,
  CHARITY,
  STATES_DATA,
  TAX,
  dataUpdatedAt,
  longDate,
} from '../lib/data';
import { usd } from '../lib/format';
import { figuresFootnote } from '../lib/render';

/*
 * A note that states a fact carries a link to the page that states it.
 *
 * Where the fact is already a number in a data file, the link is read out of
 * that file's meta block. The note and the figure then cite one page, and a
 * refresh that moves the source moves both at once.
 *
 * Every note carries a stable id. Refer to a note by that id, never by its
 * position: DATA.md and SOURCES.md both cited note numbers, both drifted when
 * the list grew, and both ended up pointing at the wrong note.
 *
 * These notes are caveats about the model. They state no figure, so they take
 * no link: estimates, scope, sameness, fungible, cumulative, federal-only,
 * returns, todays-dollars, refreshed.
 */
const stateRates = STATES_DATA.meta.fields['incomeTaxRatePct'];
const propertyRates = STATES_DATA.meta.fields['propertyTaxRatePct'];
const wageBase = TAX.meta.fields['payroll.socialSecurity.wageBase'];
const childCredit = TAX.meta.fields['credits.childTaxCredit.perChild'];
const { perChild, phaseOutStart } = TAX.credits.childTaxCredit;
const childCreditFigures =
  `${usd(perChild)} a child, falling away above ${usd(phaseOutStart.single)}` +
  ` of income, or ${usd(phaseOutStart.mfj)} on a joint return`;
const giveWell = CHARITY.meta.fields['price'];

const rentTaxShare = ASSUMPTIONS.meta.fields['propertyTax.rentTaxShare'];

/** Payroll tax incidence. No figure comes from it, so it has no meta entry. */
const incidence = {
  label: 'CBO Working Paper 2021-06, payroll tax pass-through',
  url: 'https://www.cbo.gov/publication/57089',
};

/** Property tax incidence. Cited against this page's own renter figure. */
const passThrough = {
  label: 'Carroll and Yinger, National Tax Journal 1994, on rental housing',
  url: 'https://ideas.repec.org/a/ntj/journl/v47y1994i2p295-316.html',
};
---

<section class="notes">
  <h3>Read this before you quote the number</h3>
  <ol>
    <li id="note-estimates">
      <b>These are estimates.</b> A real return depends on retirement contributions,
      itemized deductions, and dozens of other things this page does not ask about.
      It is not tax advice.
    </li>
    <li id="note-credits">
      <b>One credit is modelled, and no refund is.</b> The child tax credit comes off
      the federal line, up to {' '}
      <span set:html={childCreditFigures} />. It stops at zero: this page never shows
      a refund, and the refundable part of that credit is not counted. Nor is the
      earned income tax credit. So a head of household earning $50,000 with two
      children correctly owes no federal income tax here &mdash; but the real return
      also pays them about $1,650 back, and this page cannot show that.{' '}
      <a href={childCredit.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        {childCredit.label}
      </a>
    </li>
    <li id="note-state-tax">
      <b>State tax is approximated</b> as a flat rate on gross income. Most states use
      brackets and their own deductions, so treat this figure as a ballpark. Until you
      pick a state, the page charges the median state rate, not zero. Pick your own
      state to replace it.{' '}
      <a href={stateRates.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        {stateRates.label}
      </a>
    </li>
    <li id="note-scope">
      <b>This measures only what left your pocket</b>, not what came back. These taxes
      also pay for roads, courts, schools, defense and the like. Section three prices
      each of them against what it used to cost. Section six asks how much of the bill
      they actually are, and who else has ever done them.
    </li>
    <li id="note-sameness">
      <b>Every comparison in section six opens by saying what makes the two sides the
      same job.</b> Same statute, same unit, same buyer, same beat. A card that cannot
      make that claim is not on the page; the ones thrown out for failing it are listed
      in <code>DATA.md</code>. What remains is still not a laboratory. A public price
      often carries land taken by force of law, wages set by statute, and the review
      that permits the work. A private road or a private guard mostly serves whoever
      pays, which is the standing objection to both. Each card names its own remaining
      weakness after the figures. Read those before you quote a multiple.
    </li>
    <li id="note-fungible">
      <b>Your dollars are not traceable.</b> Money is fungible. You did not personally
      buy a missile, a prison cell or an empty desk. Every figure in section three is{' '}
      <i>your share</i> of a national total, worked out from published accounts. Nothing
      on this page claims a specific dollar of yours went to a specific thing.
    </li>
    <li id="note-cumulative">
      <b>A cumulative bill is priced against a single year of spending.</b> Every
      figure in section three divides by the same thing: the part of every federal
      dollar you pay this year. The two Afghanistan cards and the war card read low
      because of it. Those bills were run up over two decades, when the government
      spent far less each year than it does now, so measuring them against today's
      larger outlays shrinks your share. Your real share of those three is larger
      than the card says, not smaller. The other lump cards are not affected the
      same way: the equipment left behind was a single month in 2021, the two
      pandemic frauds fell in years whose outlays were close to today's, and the
      F-35 total runs to 2088, so most of it has not been spent yet.
    </li>
    <li id="note-social-security">
      <b>The Social Security card counts both halves of the Social Security tax</b>
      &mdash; 6.2% from you and 6.2% from your employer &mdash; on the same incidence
      argument used above. Medicare is not in this card. Whether the invested figure
      beats the benefit depends entirely on the years and return you pick; over a short
      run it does not. Your real benefit depends on an earnings history this page never
      asks for.{' '}
      <a href={wageBase.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        {wageBase.label}
      </a>
    </li>
    <li id="note-charity">
      <b>Charity figures are estimates, not price tags.</b> A cost per life saved is a
      model with a wide band around it, and GiveWell expects every one of these figures
      to rise over time. Read the linked analysis before you quote a number.{' '}
      <a href={giveWell.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        {giveWell.label}
      </a>
    </li>
    <li id="note-incidence">
      <b>The employer's share is counted as your money only if you accept the incidence
      argument</b> &mdash; that absent the tax, the same amount would have reached your
      wage. Most economists do; note that had it been paid to you as salary, income tax
      would have applied to it too, which this page does not model.{' '}
      <a href={incidence.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        {incidence.label}
      </a>
    </li>
    <li id="note-federal-only">
      <b>Sections three and six divide your federal tax only.</b> The bill at the top of
      this page counts federal, state and local tax together. These two sections divide
      federal spending, because Treasury publishes those accounts and no single ledger
      adds up all fifty states. So they use the federal part of your bill: income tax,
      both payroll taxes, and the employer's half when you count it. Your state, sales
      and property tax is real money and it is in the total above, but it funded no
      federal outlay and does not enlarge your share of one. Section six still understates
      the road bill: most roads are built and maintained by states and counties, not by
      Washington, and that spending is outside these accounts entirely.
    </li>
    <li id="note-property">
      <b>Property tax depends on whether you own or rent.</b> An owner is charged the
      state's effective rate on the state's median home, not on a home you told us
      about, so a cheaper or dearer house changes your own bill. A renter is charged
      the property tax inside a year of average US rent &mdash; 11 cents of every rent
      dollar, from the landlords' own trade association. That rent is a national
      average, so the renter figure does not move when you change the state, although
      the real thing does: the same research puts New Jersey at 16 cents and Arizona
      at 4. Economists also disagree about how much of a landlord's property tax a
      tenant really bears rather than the owner. Carroll and Yinger found the tax on
      rental housing is, on average, not a benefit tax, and any part of it that falls
      on the owner instead would make the renter figure smaller. Either way this is the one tax
      on the page you keep paying after the thing is bought and paid for, and an owner
      who stops paying loses the house to the county.{' '}
      <a href={propertyRates.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        {propertyRates.label}
      </a>{' '}
      <a href={rentTaxShare.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        {rentTaxShare.label}
      </a>{' '}
      <a href={passThrough.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        {passThrough.label}
      </a>
    </li>
    <li id="note-returns">
      <b>Investment returns are not guaranteed.</b> This page assumes a steady annual
      return, which no real market delivers. Actual sequences of good and bad years
      produce very different results.
    </li>
    <li id="note-todays-dollars">
      <b>Everything is in today's dollars</b> and holds your income flat, which keeps the
      comparison readable but is not how a career usually goes.
    </li>
    <li id="note-figures"><b>Figures used:</b> <span set:html={figuresFootnote()} /></li>
    <li id="note-refreshed">
      <b>Data last refreshed {longDate(dataUpdatedAt())}.</b> Every rate and price on
      this page comes from a JSON file in the repository. Each file records its own
      source and refresh date.
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>
